---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import TikiPlaceholder from '../components/TikiPlaceholder.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
import QuoteForm from '../components/QuoteForm.astro';
import { serviceSchema, breadcrumbSchema, webPageSchema } from '../lib/schema';

const path = '/miami-tiki-huts/';
const title = 'Tiki Huts Miami FL | Backyard & Poolside Thatch Huts';
const description =
  'Tiki hut builders in Miami — traditional palm-thatched chickee huts for backyards, pool decks and commercial spaces, plus re-thatching of existing structures.';

const crumbs = [{ href: '/service-areas/', label: 'Service Areas' }, { label: 'Miami Tiki Huts' }];

const schema = [
  serviceSchema({
    name: 'Tiki hut and chickee hut construction in Miami, Florida',
    description,
    path,
    serviceType: 'Tiki hut construction',
    areaServed: ['Miami, Florida', 'Miami-Dade County, Florida'],
    offers: [
      'Backyard tiki hut construction',
      'Poolside chickee huts',
      'Commercial thatched structures',
      'Tiki hut re-thatching',
    ],
  }),
  breadcrumbSchema(crumbs, path),
  webPageSchema({ path, name: title, description }),
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path={path} crumbs={crumbs} schema={schema}>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Miami tiki huts"
    title="Tiki Huts for Miami Backyards and Pool Decks"
    lede="Of all the ways to shade a Miami backyard, the thatched hut is the only one that was invented here. An open frame under a steep sabal palm roof gives you the deepest shade available and lets the breeze pass straight through it — which on a still August afternoon beside a pool is worth more than any amount of engineering."
    facts={[
      { label: 'Structures', value: 'Backyard and poolside huts, tiki bars, commercial shade' },
      { label: 'Thatch', value: 'Natural sabal palm, or synthetic where upkeep must stay low' },
      { label: 'Also offered', value: 'Re-thatching and repair of existing huts' },
      { label: 'Area', value: 'Miami and the wider Miami-Dade County' },
    ]}
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell prose">
      <h2>Why a hut works particularly well in Miami</h2>
      <p>
        Miami backyards tend to be enclosed — by walls, by fences, by neighbouring houses —
        and enclosed spaces hold heat. That is exactly the condition an open-sided structure
        is good at. A thatched roof insulates far better than its weight suggests, and because
        there are no walls beneath it, whatever air movement exists in the yard passes through
        rather than stopping at a wall.
      </p>
      <p>
        The other reason is character. A{' '}
        <a href="/pergolas/">modern pergola</a> reads as an extension of the house — precise,
        engineered, part of the building. A hut reads as a destination in the yard, somewhere
        separate that you walk out to. On a small Miami lot, that psychological difference is
        surprisingly large.
      </p>
      <p>
        The trade-off is honest and worth stating: a thatch roof is a natural, wearing
        surface, and it is not the structure to hang an outdoor kitchen, a television and
        recessed lighting off. If you want a dry, equipment-carrying room, you want a pergola
        or a cabana. If you want shade, air and something with a bit of soul in it, this is
        the better answer.
      </p>

      <h2>Where a hut goes in a Miami yard</h2>
      <p>
        Siting decides most of how good the structure will be, and in the city there are a
        few consistent considerations.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Away from the house, not against it.</strong> A hut looks and works best with
        space around it. Tucked tight against a rear wall it loses both the airflow and the
        sense of being somewhere else.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Clear of overhanging trees.</strong> Mature canopy is an asset in most Miami
        yards, but constant leaf litter and dripping shorten the life of a thatch roof
        considerably. A hut in a clearing lasts noticeably longer than one under a ficus.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Beside the pool, not over it.</strong> The classic and best placement is at
        the edge of the deck, giving you shade you can retreat to without shading the water
        itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Access matters.</strong> Thatch and framing timber have to get to the back of
        the property, and a lot of city lots have narrow side gates. It is workable — it just
        needs knowing early.
      </p>

      <h2>Permits in Miami-Dade</h2>
      <p>
        Whether a hut needs a permit depends on your building department, the structure's size
        and height, and how it is anchored. Some jurisdictions treat certain traditional
        chickee structures differently from other accessory buildings, and this is a real
        legal question about your specific address rather than something a web page should
        answer with confidence.{' '}
        <span class="placeholder-token">[PERMIT_POLICY]</span> — fill in exactly how Machin
        Solutions handles permitting so visitors get a real answer here.
      </p>

      <h2>Re-thatching an existing Miami hut</h2>
      <p>
        Plenty of Miami properties already have a hut that has quietly reached the end of its
        roof life. Thinning at the ridge or the eaves, light visible from underneath, and
        water coming through in ordinary rain rather than wind-driven downpours all point the
        same way. The frame usually stays; the roof gets rebuilt. More on that on the{' '}
        <a href="/tiki-huts/construction/">construction and re-thatching page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-sand">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Our work</p>
        <h2>Miami tiki hut photography</h2>
      </div>
      <TikiPlaceholder caption="Photographs of completed Miami tiki huts are being added here." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell form-band">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Get a tiki hut quote in Miami</h2>
        <p>
          Photograph the spot where it would go, ideally from the house and from the far side
          of the yard, and tell us roughly how much shade you want. If it is an existing hut,
          shoot the roof from underneath as well.
        </p>
        <p>
          Not sure between a hut and a modern structure? Describe how you would use the space
          and you will get a straight recommendation. The{' '}
          <a href="/tiki-huts/">tiki hut overview</a> and the{' '}
          <a href="/pergolas/">pergola pages</a> set out both sides.
        </p>
      </div>
      <QuoteForm service="Tiki Hut" city="Miami" compact />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    title="Shade, breeze and somewhere to go."
    body="Send a photograph of where it would sit. You will get an honest read on whether a hut is right for the spot."
    secondaryLabel="Tiki hut overview"
    secondaryHref="/tiki-huts/"
  />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .form-band { display: grid; gap: var(--space-l); }
  @media (min-width: 62rem) {
    .form-band { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr); gap: var(--space-2xl); align-items: start; }
  }
</style>
